faq:
  - q: "What are the best career moves for professionals in their 50s?"
    a: "Four paths that consistently produce high ROI for professionals in their 50s: (1) Senior executive consolidation — if you are at VP or SVP, position for C-suite or the most senior role in your function. (2) Fractional executive or consulting — your expertise is at its peak and the economics of independent work improve dramatically when companies can engage you for $10K–$25K per month without full-time overhead. (3) Board of directors — many public and private company boards actively seek experienced operators in their 50s. (4) Advisory relationships with growth-stage companies — equity upside with a limited time commitment."
  - q: "How do I handle ageism in the job market at 50?"
    a: "Positioning, not defensiveness. Lead your narrative with impact and current relevance — not years of experience. Your resume should cover 15 years maximum (not every job since 1995). Your LinkedIn headline should position your target, not your history. Your interview narrative should open with what you are building now, not what you have accumulated. Target companies that have senior leadership in your age cohort — those cultures value experience rather than defaulting to youth."
  - q: "Is 50 too late to start consulting or become a fractional executive?"
    a: "50 is often the ideal time to start. The factors that make consulting viable — deep expertise, a strong network, real client credibility — are at their peak in your early 50s. Most successful consultants and fractional executives launched in their 50s after accumulating 20+ years of domain depth. The transition typically takes 6–18 months to replace a full salary; having a financial runway of 12 months before starting makes the transition significantly less stressful."
  - q: "How do I get on a board of directors in my 50s?"
    a: "Three paths: (1) Angel or advisory roles at growth-stage companies — these often convert to formal board observer or board member roles. (2) Non-profit board service — builds board experience and governance credibility that translates to for-profit boards. (3) Direct outreach to search firms that place board members (Spencer Stuart, Heidrick & Struggles, Korn Ferry all have board placement practices). For public company boards, you typically need CFO, CEO, or GC-level experience or recognized functional expertise (CISO, CMO with a strong public track record)."
  - q: "What should my retirement timeline strategy look like for career decisions in my 50s?"
    a: "Treat the next 10–15 years as distinct phases, not a single glide path to retirement. Early 50s: highest earning years — maximize compensation and build financial independence aggressively. Mid-50s: begin building post-corporate income streams (consulting, board, advisory) that can continue past traditional retirement. Late 50s/early 60s: transition toward work that has high selectivity and high leverage (fewer clients, higher fees, board seats) rather than full-time employment. The goal is optionality — not a fixed retirement date."
